WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.043
- This is the Morpeth Roll.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:06.090
It's about as tall as
the Empire State Building,

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:07.920
and it's three and a half times the length

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.180
of a football field.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.840
- [Man] The Morpeth Roll,
essentially, is a thank you note

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:14.080
to Lord Morpeth, who
was the Chief Secretary

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.200
of Ireland from about 1835 to '41.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.570
- [Woman] It was estimated at the time

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:22.350
that it had 270,000 signatures.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:25.060
- Making it one of the
largest such testimonials

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:29.203
in existence provided to
George Howard Vica Morpeth.

00:00:33.110 --> 00:00:34.360
- I am Quinton Atkinson,

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:37.260
senior director of global
content at Ancestry;

00:00:37.260 --> 00:00:40.640
and this is the archival
vault at Ancestry.

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:43.940
The content team is responsible
for finding out where

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.640
documents exist throughout
the world that could provide

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:47.840
discoveries for people.

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.470
We then digitize records, and then

00:00:50.470 --> 00:00:52.653
we create searchable indexes.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.350
The Morpeth Roll is
important because it captures

00:00:57.350 --> 00:01:00.820
a group of people in
Ireland pre the 1845 famine,

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.987
but also because in 1922,
a lot of the records

00:01:03.987 --> 00:01:07.980
documenting people within
Ireland were destroyed by fire

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.400
during the Civil War of Ireland.

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.640
The Morpeth Roll really
is a census substitute

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.710
for those that lived at
that time in Ireland.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:17.507
- I am Professor Terence Dooley,

00:01:17.507 --> 00:01:21.400
principal investigator of
the Morpeth Roll Project.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:24.490
The Morpeth Roll is a
token of gratitude for the

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:27.270
reforms that have been
introduced to Ireland

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:28.810
by Lord Morpeth.

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:31.730
On the presentation of the
gift itself, Morpeth decides

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:35.780
to bring it back to his home
at Castle Howard in Yorkshire,

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.810
and there it remains for 180 years.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:42.040
- That is until 2009 when
the Lord Morpeth Roll was

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:45.860
discovered in the Castle Howard archives.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.340
- My name is Louise Walsworth-Bell.

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:51.020
I'm a paper conservator and
part of the team that worked

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.450
on the Morpeth Roll.

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:55.404
Well this is the spool on which
the Morpeth Roll was rolled,

00:01:55.404 --> 00:01:58.070
and it would have fitted into this chest.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:02.060
There are enormous difficulties
with handling a document

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:05.720
of that size, so it's really
important that we would make

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.620
the object handleable.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.200
Digitization has massively
facilitated research.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.060
- The beauty of coming to
Ancestry is that I can type in the

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:17.230
name of an ancestor I'm
looking for, we search then

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.890
all of the documents that we
have, and if we find a match,

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:21.580
you can click on the
image and actually see

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:23.550
that digital image.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:26.810
Ancestry has probably
created electronic indexes

00:02:26.810 --> 00:02:28.210
from handwritten documents

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:29.960
more than anyone else in the world.

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:34.270
We use an overhead digital
camera with lighting to

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:37.710
highlight the writing
on the page and move it

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.613
a frame at a time, so that
we can make sure we preserve

00:02:40.613 --> 00:02:45.070
every stroke of the pen,
every color variation in the

00:02:45.070 --> 00:02:45.903
parchment.

00:02:45.903 --> 00:02:48.930
At the end, after we've
captured every single image,

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:50.210
those are stitched together

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:52.323
in a document that now appears online.

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.620
- [Woman] I think the beauty
of the digitization of the roll

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:02.620
by Ancestry is it enables
access not only here within

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.020
Ireland but worldwide, so
it becomes accessible.

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:08.903
- This roll actually reaches
out now to a much wider

00:03:08.903 --> 00:03:10.620
international community.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.021
- [Man] The records that the
content team at Ancestry are

00:03:13.021 --> 00:03:16.798
searching out and digitizing
range from yearbooks

00:03:16.798 --> 00:03:20.350
with photos in them to
the military file of your

00:03:20.350 --> 00:03:23.310
great-grandfather as he
served in World War I.

00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.910
Whatever the record type, our
goal is to bring you the richest

00:03:26.910 --> 00:03:29.110
experience of connecting
with your ancestor.

00:03:30.383 --> 00:03:33.716
(Irish highlands music)

